/* Placement, before a wave, composed onto ScreenScaffold. What is left here
 * is the two things neither the frame nor a component can know: that the stat
 * cells sit in a row, and that the refusal wraps.
 *
 * FOUR RULES LEFT THIS FILE IN PHASE 8 TASK 10 AND NONE WAS REPLACED BY A
 * COPY HERE, which is the test of whether a screen composes the layer or
 * re-derives it:
 *   - the root's `padding: var(--gutter)` is the scaffold's content region.
 *   - `__slot`'s sunk fill, hairline border, row radius, padding and bottom
 *     margin were a line-by-line re-derivation of `.option-row`, which now
 *     draws the row. Every one of the six declarations matched the
 *     component's exactly.
 *   - `__blocker`'s fill, radius and padding are Theme's `.panel-coral`, and
 *     its ink is `.t-danger`.
 *   - `__start`'s top margin is the CTA row's own padding, and the button's
 *     whole treatment is Theme's `.btn-primary` - which is also what makes it
 *     the violet CTA rather than Unity's default grey.
 *
 * THE STAT ROW IS THIS FILE'S AND NOT StatCell.uss's. A cell carries half the
 * handoff's 8px gap on each of its own sides; the `flex-direction: row` that
 * puts three of them side by side belongs to whatever holds them, and a
 * component's stylesheet reaches its descendants and never its parent.
 * StatCell.uss's closing note records how that was found out.
 */
.deploy-view { flex-grow: 1; }

.deploy-view__stats { flex-direction: row; }

.deploy-view__slots { flex-direction: column; }

/* A sentence, not a label, so it wraps. Shown only when it says something -
 * DeployView.Blocker has the measurement. */
.deploy-view__blocker {
    white-space: normal;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
}
